{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"localization of quotient rings localization of quotient rings","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md","fileName":"Localization of Quotient Rings.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Localization of Quotient Rings","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:45\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Localization and Local Algebra]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Localization and Local Algebra","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"localization of quotient rings localization of quotient rings","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md","fileName":"Localization of Quotient Rings.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Localization of Quotient Rings","heading":"Localization of Quotient Rings","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Localization commutes with forming a quotient. If I is an ideal of R and S maps to a multiplicatively closed set in R/I, then S⁻¹R divided by S⁻¹I is isomorphic to the localization of R/I.\n\nThis identifies two routes—first localize and then quotient, or first quotient and then localize. Both make the same denominators invertible and turn the same ideal elements into zero.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Localization and Local Algebra","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Localization of Quotient Rings.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__no